          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "6edc0efd-655f-4180-bb39-1c7848547a51",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. This can lead to null pointer dereferencing if not properly checked.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, consider using a safer method to retrieve environment variables. Always validate and sanitize input from all untrusted data sources.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for std::getenv\n#include <string> // for std::string\n\nstd::string env_var = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (!env_var.empty()) {\n    return size_W;\n} else {\n    return sizeof(T) * size_W;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n- `<string>`: This header introduces string types, character traits and a set of converting functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4098abb3-83f1-429d-91fe-21ad1b25a078",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static buffer, which can be overwritten by subsequent calls to `std::getenv` or other functions that manipulate the environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` and other unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries. In this case, consider using a platform-specific method to retrieve environment variables, or use a configuration file or command-line arguments to pass configuration options to your program.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (buffer_size != nullptr) {\n    // Copy the value to a local buffer to prevent it from being overwritten\n    std::string buffer_size_str = buffer_size;\n    // Use the copied value\n    bytes_W = size_W;\n} else {\n    bytes_W = sizeof(T) * size_W;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C++ standard library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6edc0efd-655f-4180-bb39-1c7848547a51",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. This can lead to null pointer dereferencing if not properly checked.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, consider using a safer method to retrieve environment variables. Always validate and sanitize input from all untrusted data sources.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for std::getenv\n#include <string> // for std::string\n\nstd::string env_var = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (!env_var.empty()) {\n    return size_W;\n} else {\n    return sizeof(T) * size_W;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n- `<string>`: This header introduces string types, character traits and a set of converting functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d91b6756-9c70-4dc3-ae21-1f8fcbef18cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static memory area. Any subsequent call to `getenv` will modify the same memory area, leading to potential race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` in a multithreaded context. Instead, consider using thread-safe alternatives or ensure that the environment variables are read before any threads are created. Also, validate the input and output of all C functions, especially those that interact with system resources or perform operations on strings or buffers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the code could be to store the environment variable in a local variable at the start of the program, before any threads are created. This way, the value is safely stored and can be used later without risk of race conditions.\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nsz = buffer_size != nullptr ? lwork : sizeof(rocblas_double_complex) * lwork;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `std::getenv`\n- `rocblas` for `rocblas_double_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "168ae73c-35c7-4166-93d6-a660379321e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static memory area. If this memory is modified, it can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. If you must use `std::getenv`, ensure that the returned pointer is not directly modified and that the value is properly validated before use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nconst char* env_var = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif(env_var != nullptr) {\n    // Validate the value of env_var before use\n    sz = lwork;\n} else {\n    sz = sizeof(rocblas_float_complex) * lwork;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n- `<rocblas.h>` for `rocblas_float_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
