            {
              "id": "5ec1f06f-1a10-4175-92c2-6877350b08b2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. This function returns a pointer to a string that represents the value of the environment variable. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` or ensure that its usage is safe. Validate the input and output of `std::getenv` and ensure that the environment variable is not controlled by an attacker. Consider using safer alternatives if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would involve validating the input and output of `std::getenv`. However, since `std::getenv` is inherently unsafe, it's recommended to use a safer alternative if available. The exact fix would depend on the specific requirements of the code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `std::getenv`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
