                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `std::getenv` is flagged as it can lead to security vulnerabilities. The function `std::getenv` is used to access environment variables, which can be manipulated by an attacker to exploit the program.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that are less prone to exploitation. If you must use `std::getenv`, ensure that you validate and sanitize the input and output of the function. Also, ensure that your program does not rely on the existence, value, or state of an environment variable for its security.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* value;\nif((value = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\")) != nullptr) {\n    // Use the value\n} else {\n    // Handle the error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d91b6756-9c70-4dc3-ae21-1f8fcbef18cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static memory area. Any subsequent call to `getenv` will modify the same memory area, leading to potential race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` in a multithreaded context. Instead, consider using thread-safe alternatives or ensure that the environment variables are read before any threads are created. Also, validate the input and output of all C functions, especially those that interact with system resources or perform operations on strings or buffers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the code could be to store the environment variable in a local variable at the start of the program, before any threads are created. This way, the value is safely stored and can be used later without risk of race conditions.\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nsz = buffer_size != nullptr ? lwork : sizeof(rocblas_double_complex) * lwork;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `std::getenv`\n- `rocblas` for `rocblas_double_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d91b6756-9c70-4dc3-ae21-1f8fcbef18cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static memory area. Any subsequent call to `getenv` will modify the same memory area, leading to potential race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` in a multithreaded context. Instead, consider using thread-safe alternatives or ensure that the environment variables are read before any threads are created. Also, validate the input and output of all C functions, especially those that interact with system resources or perform operations on strings or buffers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the code could be to store the environment variable in a local variable at the start of the program, before any threads are created. This way, the value is safely stored and can be used later without risk of race conditions.\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nsz = buffer_size != nullptr ? lwork : sizeof(rocblas_double_complex) * lwork;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `std::getenv`\n- `rocblas` for `rocblas_double_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
