              "id": "4098abb3-83f1-429d-91fe-21ad1b25a078",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static buffer, which can be overwritten by subsequent calls to `std::getenv` or other functions that manipulate the environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` and other unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries. In this case, consider using a platform-specific method to retrieve environment variables, or use a configuration file or command-line arguments to pass configuration options to your program.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (buffer_size != nullptr) {\n    // Copy the value to a local buffer to prevent it from being overwritten\n    std::string buffer_size_str = buffer_size;\n    // Use the copied value\n    bytes_W = size_W;\n} else {\n    bytes_W = sizeof(T) * size_W;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C++ standard library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
