            {
              "id": "1f804d48-fa66-4c4f-8fba-741e8842d9be",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. Always validate and sanitize input data. Be aware of the potential for buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities when dealing with input data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fscanf` function with a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::ifstream` class from the C++ Standard Library to read from a file:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(mat);\nif (file >> v) {\n    read = 1;\n} else {\n    read = 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fscanf`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
